---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
---
<CaseStudy
  title="Contract Review Agent, case study · Conrad Linus Muhirwe"
  description="A durable AI workflow that analyzes every clause of a contract in parallel, then suspends until a person approves, before writing and emailing the memo. Built with Trigger.dev and Next.js."
  kicker="Case study 03 · 2026"
  headline="Contract Review Agent: fast where a model helps, and stopped where a person has to decide."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Sole builder' },
    { label: 'Stack', value: 'Next.js 15, Trigger.dev, Postgres, Prisma, Cloudflare R2, Resend' },
    { label: 'Models', value: 'Groq, Gemini, OpenRouter, OpenAI, Anthropic' },
  ]}
  links={[
    { label: 'Live app', href: 'https://contract-review-pied.vercel.app' },
    { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/LinusConradM/contract_review' },
  ]}
  ogImage="/og/contract-review.png"
  next={{ href: '/work/scorecard-explorer', title: 'Scorecard Explorer' }}
>
  <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="problem">
    <p class="label">The problem</p>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <h2 id="problem">Contract review is slow, and it should not be fully automated.</h2>
      <p>A model can read a contract clause by clause and flag risk far faster than a person. But a risk memo that goes out without a lawyer’s sign-off is a liability, and the sign-off can take minutes or weeks. A normal web request cannot wait that long, and a background job that holds a server while it waits is expensive.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="approach">
    <p class="label">The approach</p>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <h2 id="approach">One durable run per contract, with a waitpoint where the person decides.</h2>
      <p>Upload a PDF or Word file and a Trigger.dev workflow takes over. Each stage is a checkpointed task, so a crash, redeploy or eviction resumes where it stopped, with no lost or duplicated work.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure class="cs-figure">
    <div class="panel">
      <p class="label">One run, start to finish</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><span class="step-n">1</span><span>Extract text from the PDF or Word file</span></li>
        <li><span class="step-n">2</span><span>Split it into clauses</span></li>
        <li><span class="step-n">3</span><span>Analyze each clause as its own child run: risk level, explanation, ambiguous terms and recommendations. At most 5 run at once, each retried up to 5 times with exponential backoff.</span></li>
        <li class="step-key"><span class="step-n">4</span><span><strong>Wait for a reviewer.</strong> The run creates a waitpoint token, emails the owner, and suspends with no timeout, freeing its compute. A separate run sleeps for 7 days and sends a reminder if the contract is still waiting.</span></li>
        <li><span class="step-n">5</span><span>The reviewer approves or rejects clause by clause. The run resumes, writes the memo, streams it to the page and emails it.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <figcaption>Step 4 is the point of the project: nothing leaves without a person’s sign-off, and waiting costs nothing.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="providers">
    <p class="label">Model layer</p>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <h2 id="providers">Any provider, swapped without touching the pipeline.</h2>
      <p>One <code>complete()</code> call routes to Groq, Gemini, OpenRouter, OpenAI or Anthropic. With no provider pinned, it walks the configured keys in order and falls through to the next when one is rate-limited or down. Each clause run is tagged with the provider that served it and its risk band, so the dashboard can filter “all high-risk clauses” or “everything Gemini analyzed”.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .panel { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--bg-subtle); padding: 40px; }
  .steps { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin-top: 20px; }
  .steps li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 32px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 16px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .steps li:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .step-n { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); padding-top: 3px; }
  .steps .step-key { background: var(--bg-raised); margin: 0 -16px; padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-bottom-color: transparent; }
  .step-key .step-n { color: var(--accent); }
  .step-key strong { font-weight: 600; }
  :global(.cs-prose code) { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.88em; background: var(--bg-subtle); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .panel { padding: 24px; } }
</style>
